            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Fits a straight line through points - y = slope x + intercept - and also says how good the fit is: r, how closely the points follow a line (1 or -1 exactly, 0 not at all), and p, how likely a slope that strong would be by chance if there were really no trend.\n\nUse it to measure a trend and judge it at the same time: is a performer really speeding up across a phrase or is it noise; does a joint's angle really follow a fader; is a sensor drifting. A p below about 0.05 is conventionally taken as a real trend.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.linregress\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ny:\nThe values. Every input is fitted and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nx:\nWhere the values are, held. Without x they are at 0, 1, 2 ... Pairs with nan are left out; at least 3 are needed.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nslope:\nHow much y changes per step of x.\n\nintercept:\nThe value of the line at x = 0.\n\nr:\nHow closely the points follow the line: from -1 (falling exactly) through 0 to 1 (rising exactly).\n\np:\nThe chance of a slope this strong if there were no real trend. Sent first, then r, intercept and slope.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFive values rising by about 2 each step, from about 1: slope 1.99, intercept 1.04, r 0.999 - an almost perfect line - and p 0.00006, so the trend is real. A metro re-sends the values twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.polyfit fits a line (or curve) without judging it. scipy.spearmanr measures a trend that need not be straight. scipy.curve_fit fits any formula.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1000,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Fits a straight line through points - y = slope x + intercept - and also says how good the fit is: r, how closely the points follow a line (1 or -1 exactly, 0 not at all), and p, how likely a slope that strong would be by chance if there were really no trend.\n\nUse it to measure a trend and judge it at the same time: is a performer really speeding up across a phrase or is it noise; does a joint's angle really follow a fader; is a sensor drifting. A p below about 0.05 is conventionally taken as a real trend.\n\nSYNTAX:\nscipy.linregress\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ny:\nThe values. Every input is fitted and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nx:\nWhere the values are, held. Without x they are at 0, 1, 2 ... Pairs with nan are left out; at least 3 are needed.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nslope:\nHow much y changes per step of x.\n\nintercept:\nThe value of the line at x = 0.\n\nr:\nHow closely the points follow the line: from -1 (falling exactly) through 0 to 1 (rising exactly).\n\np:\nThe chance of a slope this strong if there were no real trend. Sent first, then r, intercept and slope.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nFive values rising by about 2 each step, from about 1: slope 1.99, intercept 1.04, r 0.999 - an almost perfect line - and p 0.00006, so the trend is real. A metro re-sends the values twice a second.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.polyfit fits a line (or curve) without judging it. scipy.spearmanr measures a trend that need not be straight. scipy.curve_fit fits any formula."
        },
